p a uh  segmentation algorithm to um  quantify uh brain volume from ct  so although mri is is uh  more suitable than ct for for brain  mapping uh  especially for uh quantitative  brain morphology there has been growing  ct use throughout the world especially  in the developed world  and for that reason there's been renewed  interest in head ct  segmentation and ct is also more  suitable  in cases where mri is unavailable like  ours where it's unfeasible  where it's counterindicated for  individuals who  cannot get mri scans due to pacemakers  and so forth  or if it's too costly like in many  cases in the developing world or even in  the developed world  and mri segmentation tools as many of  you known  are already technically mature but  there's been very little work  on ct brain segmentation and this is the  one gap  in the technical literature that we have  aimed to fill and  mri methods as we have demonstrated are  actually quite amenable to adaptation to  uh to ct so we have developed a ct-based  brain tissue classification  algorithm where we segment the  outer layers so the scalp  the cranium which is very nicely  segmented from ct as you might expect  and then once we get to the soft tissue  we have developed a way to  separate the gray matter from the white  matter from the css  from ct scans here is an example for one  subject  and here's the you can see the  ventricular system  which uh is actually pretty well  uh delineated you can even see the inter  thalamic adhesion  here a little bit uh you can see the  lateral ventricles the third ventricle  and this method uses a an atlas based  probabilistic labeling tool uh which is  actually quite  standard in the mri literature and has  been used for a long time  and we assign each voxel in in each ct  scan to one of three classes either gray  matter white matter and css  and we have validated this tool in  subjects in the u.s who received both  mri  nct scans for various clinical purposes  and we took  mri as being the gold standard and  and acquired also cts and  segmented their the the brain from  q1 mri using free surfer software  and then used the ct scans acquired from  the same  subjects to classify white matter gray  matter in csf  to calculate intracranial volume and to  estimate  the relative errors in segmentation  accuracy  uh taking mri as the gold standard and  this was published last year  in frontiers in neuroinformatics and  what we found  is that uh using the dice coefficient  which is a very common  metric for this kind of comparisons but  also the  the house dwarf distance which is a more  sophisticated  uh metric we found that the volume  estimation errors  uh for white matter in gray matter uh  were are actually quite reasonable so  we were able to calibrate our  segmentation  method based on mri to achieve zero  percent  average estimation error for both gray  matter  white matter and csf with a standard dev  uh  with an error range of about five  percent so that's two  two standard deviations uh above and  below the mean error  uh five percent for white matter four  percent for gray matter  and about three percent for csf and you  can see here the comparison  between the segmented brain and csf  ventricular system uh in mri versus cct  uh mri as you can imagine has a better  ability to resolve the gyro structure  but ct isn't too bad at all in terms of  finding the total brain volume which is  what we were interested in  and it also does a pretty good job of of  identifying the ventricular system  so we quantified this uh extensively we  didn't find any bias in  volume estimation errors for any of  these measures that we  used uh they all had an approximately  normal distribution  um and this was also the case for both  dice coefficients and house storage  distances uh and to compare  the chimanei to  westerners we undertook a meta-analysis  of 20 years worth of mri literature  summarizing data over almost 10 000  typically aging adults this paper was  recently published online in  neuroinformatics  and we mapped the trajectories of  brain volume gray matter white matter in  css  in uh this meta-analytic sample  meta-analyzed sample from um  from the us and europe as well as  japan and a few other places all all of  these being  industrialized populations and we traced  the  trajectories of these brain measures as  a function of age  after normalization by intracranial  volume to remove that compound  so based on those results we  compared the chimanei rates of  cross-sectional decrease  in brain volume uh  with those observed in the u.s and other  industrialized countries  and what we found is that after  controlling for sex  uh there is a cross-sectional decrease  in brain volume  of about 2.2 per decade in the chimane  compared to about 6.5 percent  per decade in the u.s and so this is  several times  a lower uh rate of atrocity in the  chimane than in the united states  suggesting that the rate of brain aging  and potentially the rate uh the risk for  cognitive impairment  and dementia are modulated to a  substantial extent  by cardiovascular disease incidents  because  the chimanei again have very low  cardiovascular disease  so what are the uh conclusions and  significance  of this well first of all  the chimanei lose brain volume quite  modestly with age  which is consistent with a low burden of  cardiovascular disease  and because cardiovascular health is  associated with brain atrophy it is very  possible that this low risk of  cardiovascular burden  which is uh potentially mediated by  high levels of exercise by a diet  low in trans fats and saturated fats  may have a highly protective effect upon  brain health and  very importantly this suggests that the  brain atrophy  can be reduced substantially by  lifestyles  associated with such low cardiovascular  disease risk  even in the presence of very high  inflammation  and what's very important about this  particular study in the chimae  is that it is 